            <ArticleTitle>Fluctuating gustatory disturbance and ophthalmodynia heralding the onset of a paramedian pontine infarction.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>983-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jocn.2010.12.015</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Lasting taste disturbance has been previously reported as a consequence of brainstem infarction, but there are no previous reports of transient gustatory sensations preceding the onset of stroke. Ophthalmodynia, presenting as &quot;salt-and-pepper&quot; eye pain, has been reported rarely. We present a 58-year-old right-handed woman who had fluctuating ophthalmodynia and taste disturbance immediately preceding a left paramedian pontine infarction. We discuss the neuroanatomical basis of taste in reference to this presentation.</AbstractText>
